#
# Apache RAT (Release Audit Tool) exclusion list for this repository.
#
# Consumed by tools/ci/rat.sh, which backs the "License audit (Apache RAT)"
# CI job. RAT 0.16.1 matches every non-comment line against the BASE NAME of
# each file and directory it walks, never against the path; a line that
# matches a directory name skips that whole subtree. Each line must be a
# valid Java regular expression (`*.md` is rejected and dropped; write
# `.*\.md`). RAT honours either its -e options or this file, never both, so
# the version-control and build-output names live here too.
#
# Only files that legitimately carry no license header are listed. A
# first-party source file is never excluded just to reach zero.

# Version control, Bazel output trees and installed Node modules.
\.git
bazel-.*
node_modules

# Heritage upstream files that never carried a header and are left as-is:
# the Apache example and test sites, the installer fragments and Debian
# maintainer scripts, the packaging make variables, the Varnish debug
# configs, the checked-in generated client JavaScript and console assets,
# and the version stamps.
mod_pagespeed_example
mod_pagespeed_test
do_not_modify
common
postinst
postrm
prerm
conffiles
make_vars\.mk
changelog\.template
control\.template
.*\.vcl
genfiles
generated
VERSION
version\.h\.in

# Chromium-derived packaging scripts (install/debian/build.sh and
# install/rpm/build.sh) carry their original BSD-style notice, which RAT's
# matchers do not recognise. Basename matching also hides two first-party
# scripts that do carry the SPDX header: pagespeed/system/console/build.sh
# and docs/spikes/avif-stream0/native/build.sh.
build\.sh

# Corpora, goldens and byte-exact test fixtures. Every first-party synthetic
# INPUT that can carry a header does (the html-parse seeds, the js-minify
# repros and bundle-fixture sources); their goldens manifests pin the
# headered bytes. What stays listed, and why:
#   units             the parity corpus units: imported third-party content
#                     plus content-addressed first-party units whose sha256
#                     is their identity (tools/parity/corpus/units)
#   goldens           generated expected-output manifests (html-parse,
#                     js-minify, parity)
#   testdata          the heritage unit-test trees (test/**/testdata):
#                     binary images and raw pixel dumps, third-party JS, and
#                     inputs whose .gold expected outputs are byte-exact
#   vendor            the vendored csstree copy under tools/parity/corpus
#   .*\.jsonl         the transition-load request mixes (data lines, no
#                     comment syntax)
#   empty.html, whitespace-only.html, text-only.html
#                     the three html-parse seeds whose test IS their entire
#                     byte content (empty input, whitespace-only input, a
#                     document with no markup at all): a comment would change
#                     what they exercise
#   whitespace.html   the IIS whitespace-only test page: its test requests a
#                     body of nothing but newlines (the heritage copy under
#                     install/mod_pagespeed_test is covered above)
#   (...)-1.(css|js)  the parity adapter_asserts and serve_diff fixtures:
#                     neg-nonimprov-1.{css,js} are byte-minimal by design (a
#                     comment would make them improvable and flip the
#                     negative-verdict rows), and the others' optimized-serve
#                     bodies feed measured baselines only the parity lane can
#                     re-harvest. Listed by name so the headered
#                     fixtures/gen_fixture_png.py is audited again.
units
goldens
testdata
vendor
.*\.jsonl
empty\.html
whitespace-only\.html
text-only\.html
whitespace\.html
(control|cors|custom-header|notransform|varyaccept|cold|cond|neg-denied|neg-flap|neg-nonimprov|neg-refresh|nonreproducible)-1\.(css|js)

# Third-party code, its generated configuration headers, and local patches.
third_party
.*\.patch
.*\.diff

# The license and notice files themselves.
LICENSE.*
NOTICE
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES

# Documentation, configuration, data and lock files: no code, no header.
.*\.md
.*\.txt
.*\.json
.*\.lock
.*\.conf
.*\.conf\.template
.*\.yaml\.template
.*-data\.template
.*\.ini
.*\.cfg
.*\.xml
.*\.config
.*\.sample
.*\.csproj
.*\.sln
.*\.props
.*\.targets
.*\.code-workspace
.*\.service
.*\.srchash
.*\.allow
GIT_COMMIT
platform_mappings
Vagrantfile
authorized_keys
\.htaccess
\.gitignore
\.gitkeep
\.gitattributes
\.dockerignore
\.nvmrc
\.cachedirs
\.source-hash
\.bazelrc
.*\.bazelrc
\.bazelignore
\.bazelversion
\.clang-format
\.clang-tidy
\.git-blame-ignore-revs
\.pre-commit-config\.yaml
pnpm-lock\.yaml
docker-compose.*\.yml
pagespeed-envoy\.yaml
github-actions-windows\.yml
